  "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a release-validation problem where focused Telegram QA rejected a verified canonical beta candidate before build because the workflow still required an obsolete code-frozen branch policy. The failure blocked candidate validation even though the requested commit exactly matched the canonical `release/2026.8.1` tip.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nCanonical beta branches are now accepted only when the requested full SHA matches the verified canonical branch tip. GitHub web-flow candidates retain provenance through unique merged-PR attribution, while the existing maintain/admin permission gate remains required; the obsolete code-frozen path is removed.\n\nThe workflow and its guard tests now use one trusted provenance helper, keeping runtime decisions and workflow-contract assertions in parity instead of duplicating the gate in multiple places.\n\n## User Impact\n\nRelease operators can run focused Telegram QA for an exact canonical beta candidate without a false provenance rejection. The repair does not weaken who may authorize a candidate or broaden accepted refs.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Head: `3d1d4afeef561a280b8e7bc5fce5d46f2aa9de54`\n- Focused proof: 125 passed, 4 skipped.\n- Green gates: actionlint, zizmor, ShellCheck, OpenGrep, format, diff check, and `check:changed`.\n- Hosted Testbox proof: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/31568390898\n- Original focused QA rejection: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/31518395746 — the verified canonical `release/2026.8.1` tip was rejected before build by the obsolete code-frozen branch policy.\n- Pre- and post-rebase autoreview: clean. Secret scan: clean.\n",
